3.1     The security of the office is at the same risk if a person
        were to bring any DOS/MOS disk into the office, press CTRL-C 
        during the boot sequence or indeed run another application
        in a background task (such as Lotus 1-2-3) as I had assumed 
        they would probably do.

        However, it is possible to remove the exit facility from the
        diagnostics disk.

3.2     The MMI (HCI human computer interface) was developed for the
        USPS application and used (without any training) by
        42,000 clerks.  I can only assume that they find it more
        intuitive than the 'reviewer'.

3.3     The term 'BOS' (Back Office System) is used throughout
        UNISYS documentation and is the term used in both the
        demonstration documentation and contract.  'BOP' (Back Office
        Processor) is a term invented by iT.  UNISYS use the term BOP
        to refer to the Back Office Printer.

3.4     The automatic first pass is not part of the USPS diagnostics
        and was specifically requested by POCL.

3.5     It is possible to modify the diagnostics so that the
        tests are interruptible. This facility is not currently
        available as there is a requirement to run through all tests
        automatically.

3.6     It is possible to modify the diagnostics so that the printing
        is disabled.  However if the error is caused by a temporary
        fault i.e. OFF LINE/SWITCHED OFF. the printing will resume as
        soon as the fault is corrected. This is not the case if the
        software assumes a permanent fault.

3.7     As the software has been modified from the USPS diagnostics
        it has used the same keys.  These could however be changed.

3.8.1   These are supposed to be internal loopback tests only. 
        I would suggest that the insertion and removal of the
        connectors/loop-back connectors may be beyond the scope of
        the tests performed by a clerk.

3.8.2   This is an error in the software and has already been
        corrected.

3.9     An external loop-back connector on the parallel port would
        only test the data lines not the signal lines.

3.10    The floppy disk tests perform a read/write/format test in one
        area of the disk (unused by the diagnostics) If the diagnostics 
        has been loaded, it is possible to access different areas of the 
        disk and therefore only needs to test the three functions above.

        Stopping the operation part way through has no effect on the
        diagnostics disk as the area is not used.

3.11    Bad blocks are a software issue and therefore not tested/corrected 
        in HARDWARE diagnostics.

3.12    see 3.10

3.13    The keyboard legends could be moved into a separate file this
        would have to be re-released just as the software would.

3.13.2  The shifted function keys produce the same code as the normal
        function keys as specified by iT's ECCO+ team.

3.14    The tests require user confirmation and therefore test the
        printed output.

        The hardware diagnostics do not test software (device drivers).

3.15    The test suggested test does not test if the clock is moving
        at the correct speed.  The only errors likely with a
        semiconductor clock are :
        
        1. STOPPED

        2. Running very slightly out of time.

        A test for condition 1 is performed adequately by the
        diagnostics.

        To test for condition 2 would require monitoring a calibrated
        source (i.e. RUGBY RADIO) and comparing it (over a number of hours) 
        to the internal clock.

3.16    The user diagnostics although supplied with the same batch
        file as the factory diagnostics, NEVER contain the
        'semaphore' file and therefore NEVER format the hard disk.
        As the reviewer's hard disk was not formatted the first time
        the diagnostics was run. I must assume that this was obvious.

3.17    The hardware diagnostics DO NOT test software.

3.18    The changes mentioned are to correct the error noted in 3.8.2.
        The other changes are purely cosmetic and therefore would not
        require a new release to POCL.

